     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is above the safe maximum.** 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg of metformin a day. The labeled maximum is 2,550 mg a day for immediate-release (usually 850 mg three times daily) and 2,000 mg a day for extended-release. So this order is double the safe ceiling. At that level the risks include serious stomach and bowel side effects and lactic acidosis, a rare but potentially fatal complication. It may be a transcription error, for example 1700 mg a day split into doses, or 850 mg three times daily.\n\n**2. Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber.** Since you're not a clinician, I shouldn't enter a medication order on your instruction, whatever the dose. Prescribing has to come from someone with prescribing privileges who is responsible for this patient's care. That also keeps the order's accountability and audit trail intact.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team so they can confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves.\n- If there's a chance the patient is already getting this dose, flag it to the team or the pharmacist right away.\n\nI'm happy to help the prescribing clinician enter a corrected order once they confirm it."
      }
     ],
